<Base title="Privacy Policy" description="Privacy Policy of Talha M Blog: what information we collect, how we use it, and your choices.">
  <section class="article-head"><h1>Privacy Policy</h1></section>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><em>Last updated: October 15, 2026</em></p>

    <p>Your privacy matters to us. This Privacy Policy explains, in simple words, everything about information on Talha M Blog (talhablog.com): what is collected when you visit, read, comment or subscribe, how that information is used, which services help us run this website, what choices you have, and who you can contact with questions. By using this website, you agree to the practices described on this page.</p>

    <h2>Who we are</h2>
    <p>Talha M Blog is an independent news and information website that explains stories in simple English. The website is operated by Talha M, based in Pakistan. In this policy, "we", "us" and "our" mean Talha M Blog; "you" means any visitor of this website.</p>

    <h2>The simple summary</h2>
    <p>Reading our stories never needs an account, a sign-in, or personal details. This website also offers two optional features: a comments section under our stories, and a free newsletter for readers who want new stories by email. Both are explained in full below. Advertising on this website is provided by Google (Google AdSense), and the website is delivered and protected by Cloudflare. We never sell personal information to anyone, we never add anyone to a marketing list without their own clear request, and we do not build personal profiles of our visitors.</p>

    <h2>Information you give us directly</h2>
    <p>Personal information reaches us only when you choose to send it yourself, in one of these three ways:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Email messages:</b> If you email us at contact@talhablog.com, we receive your email address, your name if it appears in your email account, and the contents of your message. We use this information only to read and reply to your message and to deal with the matter you wrote about — for example, checking a correction you reported. We do not use email addresses sent to us for advertising, and we do not add them to any marketing list. If you ask us to delete an email conversation, write to us and we will remove it from our mailbox.</li>
      <li><b>Comments:</b> If you leave a comment under a story, we receive the name, email address and (if you add it) website link that you type into the comment box, together with the comment itself. This is explained in detail in the Comments section below.</li>
      <li><b>Newsletter:</b> If you subscribe to our newsletter, we receive your email address through our newsletter provider, Kit. This is explained in detail in the Newsletter section below.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Comments — how they work</h2>
    <p>Most stories on this website have a comments section at the bottom of the page, where readers can share their views. If you use it, please understand the following:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>To comment, you type a name, an email address, and optionally a website link. You do not create an account and there is no password.</li>
      <li>Your name, your comment, and the date and time are shown publicly under the story, where every visitor can read them. If you added a website link, it is shown with your name.</li>
      <li>Your email address is <b>never</b> shown publicly. It is kept private and is used only to run the comments section — for example, to recognise returning commenters and to keep spam and abuse away.</li>
      <li>Like almost every comments system on the internet, our comments service also records technical details with each comment, such as the commenter's IP address and browser type. These are used only for security and spam protection, and they are not shown publicly.</li>
      <li>Comments are stored in a secure online database operated for us by our hosting providers (the comments service runs on Vercel, with its database hosted by Neon). The comments box's own program and design files are delivered to your browser through the unpkg content delivery network. We can read, approve, reply to and remove comments.</li>
      <li>We may remove comments that are spam, abusive, illegal, or clearly unrelated to the story. Removed comments are deleted from the database.</li>
      <li>If you want your own comment removed, email us from the same email address you used for the comment, tell us which story it is under, and we will delete it.</li>
      <li>Please do not post personal information about yourself or anyone else inside a comment, because comments are public.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Newsletter — how it works</h2>
    <p>Readers can join our free email newsletter through the sign-up box shown on our story pages. If you use it, please understand the following:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The newsletter is run for us by our email provider, <b>Kit</b> (kit.com). When you enter your email address in the sign-up box and press Subscribe, your email address is sent to Kit, which stores and processes it for us.</li>
      <li>Joining works by double confirmation: after signing up, Kit sends you a confirmation email, and your subscription starts only when you click the confirmation link in that email. This makes sure nobody can subscribe another person's address.</li>
      <li>We use your email address for one purpose only: sending you emails about new stories published on Talha M Blog. We do not use newsletter addresses for advertising lists of other companies, and we never sell or rent them.</li>
      <li>Every newsletter email contains an unsubscribe link at the bottom. You can leave the list at any time with one click, and sending to you stops. You can also email us and ask to be removed, and we will remove you.</li>
      <li>Kit may record basic technical information connected with the emails, such as whether an email was delivered, in line with Kit's own privacy policy. Kit's handling of data is described at kit.com/privacy.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Information collected automatically</h2>
    <p>Like almost every website on the internet, some technical information is recorded automatically when your browser loads our pages. This happens so the website can be delivered to you, kept secure, counted in simple totals, and protected against attacks and abuse. This information can include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your IP address (the internet address of your connection).</li>
      <li>The type of device, browser and operating system you use.</li>
      <li>The pages you open on this website and the time of your visit.</li>
      <li>The website or link that brought you to us, where your browser shares it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Our website is delivered through Cloudflare, a global network that speeds up websites and protects them from attacks. Cloudflare processes this technical information on our behalf for security and performance purposes. We do not use this information to identify individual readers.</p>

    <h2>Search on this website</h2>
    <p>The search box on this website works only inside our own pages: it looks through the titles and text of our published stories and shows matching results. What you type into the search box is used to find stories for you; it is not used to build a profile of you.</p>

    <h2>Installing our app (PWA)</h2>
    <p>Your browser may offer to install Talha M Blog as an app on your phone or computer, or to add it to your home screen. If you install it, your own browser stores copies of the website's files (such as the design, the logo and recently opened pages) on your own device. This makes the website open faster and lets recently read pages open even with a weak connection. The stored copies stay on your device, under your control; installing the app does not send us any extra personal information, and you can remove the app and its stored files at any time through your device or browser settings.</p>

    <h2>Cookies — what they are</h2>
    <p>A cookie is a small text file that a website or its partners store in your browser. Cookies help websites remember choices (such as light or dark mode), understand how pages are being read, and — in the case of advertising — show ads that are more relevant to the person seeing them. You can block or delete cookies at any time in your browser settings; the stories on this website will still be readable.</p>

    <h2>Cookies and similar storage used on this website</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Your display choice:</b> If you switch this website between light and dark mode, that single choice is saved inside your own browser (in what is called local storage). It is not a tracking cookie, it is not sent to us, and it never leaves your device for our benefit.</li>
      <li><b>Comments form:</b> If you leave a comment, the comments box may remember the name, email and website link you typed, saved inside your own browser, so you do not have to type them again next time. This stays in your browser and you can clear it through your browser settings.</li>
      <li><b>Your consent choice:</b> If you answer the consent message described below, your choice is stored so the message does not keep asking you on every page.</li>
      <li><b>Advertising cookies:</b> This website is supported by advertising provided by Google (Google AdSense). As explained in the next section, Google and its partners may place and read cookies in your browser in order to serve and measure advertisements.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Apart from the items above, this website itself does not set its own tracking cookies and does not run any other advertising or analytics cookie system of its own.</p>

    <h2>Google AdSense and advertising cookies</h2>
    <p>Talha M Blog uses Google AdSense, an advertising service operated by Google, to show advertisements on our pages. Advertising keeps this website free for readers. Please understand the following, which is important:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Third-party vendors, including Google, use cookies to serve ads based on your prior visits to this website and to other websites on the internet.</li>
      <li>Google's use of advertising cookies enables Google and its partners to serve ads to you based on your visits to this site and/or other sites.</li>
      <li>These ads are selected automatically by Google's systems. We do not choose which individual advertisement you see, and the appearance of an advertisement on this website does not mean that we personally recommend or endorse the product, service or company being advertised.</li>
      <li>You can opt out of personalised advertising by visiting Google's Ads Settings page: <a href="https://adssettings.google.com" rel="noopener" target="_blank">adssettings.google.com</a>. If you opt out, you may still see advertisements, but they will not be personalised using those cookies.</li>
      <li>You can also learn about opting out of cookies from many advertising companies at once at <a href="https://www.aboutads.info/choices/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">aboutads.info/choices</a>.</li>
      <li>For more detail on how Google itself uses information from sites that use its services, see Google's own explanation at <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites" rel="noopener" target="_blank">policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Your consent choices (EEA, UK and Switzerland)</h2>
    <p>If you visit from the European Economic Area, the United Kingdom or Switzerland, you are shown a consent message provided through Google when you arrive. This message asks whether you agree to the use of your personal data and cookies for purposes such as personalised advertising. You can accept, refuse, or open "Manage options" to choose purpose by purpose. Your choice is remembered, and you can change or withdraw it later through the privacy and cookie settings link connected with that message. If you refuse, you can still read the whole website; advertisements may still appear, but they will not be personalised for you.</p>

    <h2>Share buttons and links to other websites</h2>
    <p>Our pages include share buttons for services such as WhatsApp, Facebook, X (Twitter) and Telegram, and a button that copies the page link. These buttons are simple links: nothing is sent to those services unless you tap a button yourself. Once you follow a share button or any other link and leave Talha M Blog, this Privacy Policy no longer covers you; the other website's or app's own privacy policy applies instead. The same applies to links inside our articles — for example, to original news reports, official pages, sources we mention, and our own social media profiles such as Instagram. We are not responsible for the privacy practices or the content of external websites, and we encourage you to read their policies if you are concerned.</p>

    <h2>How long information is kept</h2>
    <p>Email messages are kept only as long as they are needed to deal with the matter you wrote about. Comments stay with the story they belong to, unless you ask us to remove yours or we remove it under our comment rules. Newsletter subscriptions are kept until you unsubscribe or ask us to remove you. Technical logs held by our delivery and security providers are kept for limited periods according to those providers' own policies, after which they are deleted or anonymised by them.</p>

    <h2>How information is protected</h2>
    <p>This website is served over a secure HTTPS connection, which encrypts the connection between your browser and the website. The services that run our comments and newsletter are protected by the security measures of their providers, and access to the small amount of information we hold is limited to the people who run this website. We take reasonable steps to protect information. At the same time, no method of sending or storing information over the internet can honestly be promised to be one hundred percent secure, and we do not make that promise.</p>

    <h2>Children's privacy</h2>
    <p>Talha M Blog is a general-audience news website. It is not directed at young children, and we do not knowingly collect personal information from children. If a parent or guardian believes a child has sent us personal information — for example, through a comment or the newsletter — please contact us and we will delete it.</p>

    <h2>Your choices and rights</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>You can read this entire website without giving us any personal information.</li>
      <li>You can block or clear cookies in your browser settings at any time.</li>
      <li>You can opt out of personalised advertising through Google's Ads Settings, as explained above.</li>
      <li>If you are shown the consent message, you can accept, refuse, or fine-tune your choices, and you can change them later.</li>
      <li>If you have emailed us and want to know what information of yours we hold, or you want it deleted, write to us at the address below and we will handle your request.</li>
      <li>If you left a comment and want it removed, email us from the same email address you used for the comment and we will delete it.</li>
      <li>If you joined the newsletter, you can unsubscribe at any time using the link at the bottom of any newsletter email, or by asking us to remove you.</li>
      <li>We never sell personal information, so there is nothing of yours being sold to opt out of.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>We may update this Privacy Policy from time to time, for example if we add a new service or if the law changes. When we do, the new version will be posted on this page and the "Last updated" date at the top will change. We encourage regular readers to check this page occasionally.</p>

    <h2>Contact us about privacy</h2>
    <p>If you have any question about this Privacy Policy or about how your information is handled, please email us at <a href="mailto:contact@talhablog.com">contact@talhablog.com</a> or use our <a href="/contact/">Contact page</a>. We will do our best to answer clearly and honestly.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
